12.3 Falsifying universal quantification \(\forall\)
An even stronger version to conjunction judgments are universal quantification judgments. These make judgments simultaneously about several claims. To do this we think of a sentence \(P\) that contains a variable \(x\) such that when we substitute for \(x\) we get proposition. The deserves its own name.

A predicate is a string \(P\) containing a variable \(x\) (or variables) such that when we substituting we get a proposition in the judgment system.

Here are some examples of predicates.

\(p\) is a prime number.
\(x\) is a mammal.
\(541\) is the 100th prime number.

Notice in the last line we have a predicate without variables. That allowed as well.
Bringing thing back to universal quantification, the clue is usually to involve the words “all” or “every” though at times it can be more subtle such as omitting to say where the variable comes from.

For all integers \(n\), \(n^2\geq 0\)
Every bat is a mammal.
Every applicant will have their application reviewed. (Falsifiable, but still a universally quantified proposition).
Cars have four wheels. (Here we have omitted to say “all cars” or “every car” but it is implied by the style of English grammar, or least English conversation in the USA, forgive me New Zealander’s if you would not be so rash.)


The proposition “For all \(x\), \(P(x)\)” justifies for each substitute \(c\) for \(x\) the resulting proposition \(P(c)\) is valid. We use the symbol \(\forall\) with the following grammar \[
\tag{$\forall$-Language}
\langle universal \rangle ::= \forall \langle variable\rangle.\langle predicate \rangle
\] and eliminate it thus: \[
\tag{$\forall$-Elim.}
\begin{array}{rl}
    & \forall x.P(x) \\
\text{get}_c    & \phantom{\forall}c \\
\hline
& \phantom{\forall x.}P(c)
\end{array}
\] The use of the name \(\text{get}_c\) for the elimination rule is to signify more precisely for which value \(c\) are we using the claim.
In falsifying a universal quantification we need only find even one \(c\) such that \(P(c)\) is invalid. This is known as a counterexample.

The diagram form of \(\forall x.P(x)\) is quite similar to the diagram for \(\wedge\)-elimination. The difference is that we have a variable \(x\) and we can substitute any value for \(x\) to get a proposition. So the diagram is more like a tree with many branches.
graph LR
    FORALL["∀x.P(x)"] --&gt;|"get_c"| P["P(c)"]






NoteRandom facts



Using \(\forall x.P(x)\) is in line with our earlier discussion of Church’s \(\lambda\)-calculus where we wrote \(\lambda x.M\). In fact some authors would prefer we wrote \(\forall x. P\) instead of \(\forall x.P(x)\) and I see their point. But I have taught so many students that I think correctness should give way to clarity on this point. Also, I want to call attention to the fact that \(\forall\) is an upside down \(A\) to signify “all”, but as a happens it also looks like a bull with horns. In fact, the alphabet originally drew the letter \(A\) this way as a bull’s head  because the ancient word for bull was “eleph” which starts out sounding like the letter “A”. It was flipped upside down around 800 BC to draw attention to a formal change in the alphabet and signal to readers they were using the new alphabet.
Welcome home eleph , math is happy to have you back!



Falsify the proposition “All dogs have four legs” by finding a counterexample. Detail the predicate and the system of judgment being used.

Software was somewhat late to including universal quantification in general but there are several idioms and data types in programming languages that give partial support.

RecordsHeterogeneous listsTemplatesDependent types


In many languages you must name all the substitutes for a variable in a predicate creating what is known as “record” or “structure”. The meaning of each entry depends on the label. For example, the following record Dog gives one type of meaning for legs (a number) and another for name (a string). For labels that are not included the meaning is understood to be a failure.
struct Dog {
    int legs;
    char *name;
};
jasper = Dog{3, "Jasper"};       // Jasper is a three-legged dog
printf("%d\n", jasper.legs);     // Jasper is evidence for 3 legs
printf("%d\n", jasper.vaccine);  // !Fails, vaccine is not a label.


Many languages allow a lists of different data so that the position in the list is all we need to supply. When the data is the evidence for a proposition this provides a form of \(\forall i. P(i)\) where \(i\) is the positions in the list.
heterogeneous = ["Hello World", 541, 3.14, True]
print(heterogeneous[0])  # prints "Hello World"
print(heterogeneous[1])  # prints 541
print(heterogeneous[2])  # prints 3.14
print(heterogeneous[3])  # prints True


In C++ and other languages it is possible to prescribe code that will be copied and variables replaced called template. Variables are in the bracket form &lt;var&gt;. In these systems we can sometimes include numbers as part of the template giving an effect similar to quantifying over numbers \(\forall i.P(i)\). For example,
#include &lt;array&gt;
#include &lt;cstddef&gt;

template &lt;std::size_t N&gt;
using Vec = std::array&lt;float, N&gt;;

template &lt;std::size_t N&gt;
Vec&lt;N&gt; range() {
    Vec&lt;N&gt; v{};
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i &lt; N; ++i) {
        v[i] = static_cast&lt;float&gt;(i);
    }
    return v;
}

// usage:
Vec&lt;4&gt; v = range&lt;5&gt;();  // Fails!  4 is not 5
Vec&lt;5&gt; v = range&lt;5&gt;();  // {0.0,1.0,2.0,3.0,4.0}
std::size_t n = ...; // some size in the future.
Vec&lt;n&gt; v = range&lt;n&gt;();  // Works for any n


Full throated support for universal quantification is found in languages with dependent types. They feature a syntax such as forall x. P(x) or def f(x:X) :P(x). Idris is such a language.
range : (n:Nat) -&gt; Vect n Int
range Z = []
range (S k) = range k ++ [k]
We shall see more on this in the next chapter when we cover types officially.

27.3 Pattern Making
Now let’s take the perspective of the letter reader. When Codaydeh receives a transmission he knew just what to do and starts taking in line by line text. There is only once source of the data but many uses. The data could be broken into words, then characters, those could be printed in some font, shown an a screen, saved to a file, who knows. But we don’t need to know the use to know the work is to take this single source and divide it up into parts we can use. That means we can focus on the many elimination rules but need only one introduction rule. The trick is to reverse the semantics, all arrows go the opposite direction. Let us start by reversing the operators.

Co-operators of Co-induction
Suppose we expect that our algebra packaged data up using operators \(\mathbb{U}^{d_1}\sqcup \cdots \sqcup \mathbb{U}^{d_n}\to \mathbb{U}\). Then when we get a data from \(\mathbb{U}\) then we should expect it was made from one of the terms \(\mathbb{U}^{d_i}\). So we just need to reverse this. That is, we need a function \(\mathbb{U}\to \mathbb{U}^{d_1}\sqcup \mathbb{U}^{d_2}\). How do we make it? Let make \(n=2\) to keep things easy.
Step 1. Remember that \(\mathbb{U}^{d_1}\sqcup \mathbb{U}^{d_2}\) is type based on OR, and so it has 2 introduction rules, one for \(\iota_1:\mathbb{U}^{d_1}\to \mathbb{U}^{d_1}\sqcup \mathbb{U}^{d_2}\) and \(\iota_2:\mathbb{U}^{d_2}\to \mathbb{U}^{d_1}\sqcup \mathbb{U}^{d_2}\).
Step 2. We need to test the incoming \(u:\mathbb{U}\) to know if it came from the left \(\mathbb{U}^{d_1}\) or right \(\mathbb{U}^{d_2}\). Whichever one it is we can make a function \(\mathbb{U}\to \mathbb{U}^{d_1}\) or \(\mathbb{U}\to \mathbb{U}^{d_2}\) and then compose it with \(\iota_1\) or \(\iota_2\).



Screenshot 2026-02-24 at 10.23.23 PM.png


The point is we can get form \(\mathbb{U}\) to \(\mathbb{U}^{d_1}\sqcup \mathbb{U}^{d_2}\).
Now the precise mechanics to allow this is subtle and requires the programming language called “destructors” or “unapply”. In particular there is not function \(\mathbb{U}\to \mathbb{U}^{d_1}\) because not every input comes from that side. We test to be sure but it means that this side of our program is technically not “tapeable function”. So there is a special process within the logic that lets us use this as “partial function”. You can ignore this, but it feels right to tell you that sometimes logic needs to go deeper.
Fortunately most modern programming languages include some nice notation to hide the mechanics and make it easy to use. Here’s a program that hides the work. We have a file that can have an End Of File (EOF) or we can have a character a:Char and a file tail:File and we want to add a to the file at position p. This would formally be the following function type.
\(\displaystyle \text{EOF}: \mathbb{U}^0\to \mathbb{U}\) and \(\displaystyle \text{Insert}(a,p) : \mathbb{U}^1\to \mathbb{U}\)
which means when unified as a single operator we have
\(displaystyle \text{all-ops} : \left(\mathbb{U}^0\sqcup \bigsqcup_{a:\text{Char}} \bigsqcup_{p:\text{Int}} \mathbb{U}^1\right)\longrightarrow \mathbb{U}\)
Thus the co-operators will reverse this array
\(displaystyle \text{co-ops} : \mathbb{U} \longrightarrow \left(\mathbb{U}^0\sqcup \bigsqcup_{a:\text{Char}} \bigsqcup_{p:\text{Int}} \mathbb{U}^1\right)\)
Let’s call this co-operator unpack and see how close we can get with software.

OCaml ExampleScala ExampleJava ExampleAgda Example


type file =
| EOF
| Insert of char * int * file

let unpack (f : file) : (file array, (char * int) * file array) Either.t =
match f with
| EOF -&gt; Either.Left [||]
| Insert (c, p, rest) -&gt; Either.Right ((c, p), [|rest|])
This approach shows the two possible returns, a File array, or a file array indexed by a character and a position. However it falls short in that it does not have the lengths of these arrays which are in fact known to us. We can achieve this by removing the array type and using the specific number.


enum File:
case EOF
case Insert(c: Char, p: Int, rest: File)

def unpack(f: File): Either[Array[File], ((Char, Int), Array[File])] =
f match
case File.EOF =&gt; Left(Array.empty[File])
case File.Insert(c, p, rest) =&gt; Right(((c, p), Array(rest)))


import java.util.Optional;

sealed interface File permits File.EOF, File.Insert {
record EOF() implements File {}
record Insert(char c, int p, File rest) implements File {}
}

class FileUnpack {
static Object unpack(File f) {
return switch (f) {
case File.EOF eof -&gt; eof;
case File.Insert ins -&gt; new CharFilePair(ins.c(), ins.p(), ins.rest());
};
}

record CharFilePair(char c, int p, File rest) {}
}


open import Data.Char
open import Data.Nat
open import Data.Sum using (_⊎_; inj₁; inj₂)
open import Data.Product using (_×_; _,_)

data File : Set where
EOF    : File
Insert : Char → ℕ → File → File

unpack : File → File ⊎ ((Char × ℕ) × File)
unpack EOF = inj₁ EOF
unpack (Insert c p rest) = inj₂ ((c , p), rest)

Transitive
By far the most common rule is transitivity. \[
\tag{Transitivity}
\begin{array}{rc}
P &\vdash Q\\
Q &\vdash R\\
\hline
P &\vdash R
\end{array}
\]


Exchange
Next in line is exchange which says in effect that we need not worry about the order of our premises. In temporal logics (logics that deal with time) and spacial logics (logic dealing with location) this sort of rule may in fact be replaced by ones that pay attention to the need for sequencing. \[
\tag{Exchange}
\frac{P,Q \vdash R}{Q, P \vdash R}
\]


Identity
Except for systems that may need to meticulously tracking resources, it is generally accepted that we a premise can justify itself and that one appearance of the premise is as good as several. \[
\tag{Identity}
P \vdash P
\] \[
\tag{Contraction}
\frac{
    P, P \vdash Q
}{
    P \vdash Q
}
\]


Monotonicity
For many arguments it is ok to include a premise that never gets used. \[
\tag{Monotonicity}
\frac{
    P \vdash Q
}{
    P,R \vdash Q
}
\] This rule also goes by the name thinning or weakening.
While monotonicity is a common rule in math and science there are considerable situations where this rule is rejected. Such non-monotonic logics are often used when detailing how our knowledge evolves with new evidence. We begin with default judgments but upon learning of new information \(R\) we need to adapt. This is a common requirement in error analysis, probability, and the more general subject of a posteriori analysis (the logic that deals with empirical origins of facts).

When we reason inductively we look at evidence we have, conjecture about possible options, and compile a strategy that works through the cases we identify. When our concerns are computational we need all our evidence to come in the form of data, data of various types. And the strategy is to develop transformations of our data. That means functions of a special shape we call operators. All the operators together form the so-called “algebra” of our induction. Many times the operators will be used over and over again, in what we call “recursion”, reflecting the idea that sometimes complexity comes from repeating simpler ideas over and over again. Think of \(4n\); that is simply \(n+(n+(n+n)))\) so it is addition repeated 3 times. Let’s begin with an archetype situation where we need induction.



Induction & Coconspirators


Once we create data inductively we will need a strategy to take such complex data apart, and for that we reverse the constructions in a process known sometimes as “coalgebra”. Instead of operators we reverse the direction and have “cooperators” and instead of recursion we make streams of “corecursion”. But all this is too abstract to take in without some examples. Coalgebras will come next lesson!

